  <h2 id="about">About FunNow Manager</h2>
  <p>FunNow Manager is the back office for FunNow’s partner merchants. Here they set the time slots they sell, handle orders, redeem bookings, get order notifications, and set up FunNow Booking.</p>

  <h3>Goal</h3>
  <p>When merchants manage their time slots, they mostly need to answer two questions: does this slot need a discount? And can customers still book at the discounts already set up? The old interface couldn’t answer either, so merchants had to work it out from their orders. The goal of this redesign was that merchants open their slot settings and <span class="hl">instantly see which slots need a discount, and whether the ones they’ve set up can still sell</span>.</p>

  <h3>Challenges</h3>
  <p>Merchants can set up three types of time slots: <strong>recurring</strong> (fixed weekly slots), <strong>special</strong> (holidays or one-off changes) and <strong>flash sale</strong> (some open spots offered at a special price). The three serve different purposes, but in the old interface they looked almost the same and shared one inventory pool. To help merchants tell which type each slot is and how many spots are left, <span class="hl">we first had to untangle the calculation rules behind them</span> before deciding what the screen should show.</p>

  <h3>My role</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Research and definition</strong>: gathered merchants’ setup problems and needs, raised the shared-inventory problem, and <span class="hl">wrote the inventory calculation logic for all three slot types</span></li>
    <li><strong>Design</strong>: information architecture, wireframes and UI for the time slot settings page</li>
    <li><strong>Delivery</strong>: aligned the calculation rules with the engineering team and made sure the rules and the UI matched</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="muted">The redesign launched on April 10, 2024.</p>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="research">Research</h2>
  <p>I interviewed partner merchants across industries, including restaurants and beauty, and checked with account managers, operations and customer service about the setup questions merchants ask most. Merchants cared most about two things: <strong>how many spots are left</strong>, and <strong>whether a discount is currently on sale</strong>. In the old version, both took a long time to find.</p>
  <OldBackend />
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Problem in the old version</th><th>What merchants had to do</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Couldn’t see how many had sold</td><td>Counted remaining spots from the order list themselves, and still couldn’t tell whether a slot had sold out</td></tr>
      <tr><td>All three slot types shared one inventory pool, so once a flash sale sold out, the whole slot showed as full</td><td>Had open spots they couldn’t sell; some merchants simply set the entire day as a flash sale</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="execution">Design decisions</h2>

  <h3>Decision 1 · Available and sold, visible at a glance</h3>
  <SlotCards />
  <CardAnatomy />
  <p>What merchants most want to know when they open their slot settings is “can this still sell?” So each slot card now shows the number sold and the booking limit, and merchants no longer have to count. If a slot is selling slowly, they know whether to add a flash sale.</p>
  <p><strong>Time on top, type by color</strong>: in the old card, the slot name sat at the top and the slot type was always spelled out, so merchants had to read the text to know which type it was. The new card always shows the time at the top and uses a large color block for the slot type, <span class="hl">so merchants can tell slot types apart by color without reading</span>.</p>

  <h3>Decision 2 · Rework the inventory rules so flash sales do what they’re meant to</h3>
  <div class="small-fig"><Figure src={stockRules} alt="Slot type definitions and inventory calculations for four scenarios (in Chinese, details blurred)" /></div>
  <p><strong>The original problem</strong>: letting merchants set their own pricing for each time slot is at the core of FunNow. A flash sale is meant to take some unsold spots and sell them at a special price; once those sell out, the slot goes back to selling at the regular price. But in the old version all three slot types shared one inventory pool, so <span class="hl">once a flash sale sold out, the whole slot showed as full, and customers couldn’t even book at the regular price</span>. For example, if a restaurant had 20 seats and put 5 on a flash sale, once those 5 were booked, the remaining 15 seats couldn’t be sold. Some merchants simply set the entire day as a flash sale, and the “flash sale” label on the customer side lost its meaning.</p>
  <p><strong>New rules</strong>: after I raised this problem, the PM defined what each slot type is for: special slots must never oversell, and flash sales should maximize how much gets sold. I then turned this into the actual calculation logic for the engineering team to build:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Flash sale: separate inventory</strong>. Once it sells out, the slot automatically goes back to its special or recurring slot and keeps selling</li>
    <li><strong>Special and recurring: shared sales count</strong>. When a special slot is deleted or edited, spots already sold still count against the recurring slot’s limit</li>
  </ul>
  <Guide uid="g-mgr-1-en" tone="green" title="Trade-off: accurate inventory matters more than flexible settings">
    <p>The cost of a shared sales count is that when a merchant deletes a special slot, spots already sold don’t come back. For example, if a recurring slot has 99 spots and 30 sell while a special slot is active, deleting the special slot leaves 69, not 99. We chose to make sure the same slot can never oversell; merchants who want to sell beyond that limit can use a flash sale, which has its own inventory.</p>
  </Guide>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="results">Results</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Merchants see the number sold right on the slot card, instead of counting from the order list</li>
    <li>When a flash sale sells out, the slot goes back to its original setting and keeps selling, so open spots no longer go to waste</li>
    <li>Merchants can tell slot types apart by color, so they can distinguish regular slots from promotions without reading</li>
  </ul>

  <hr />

  <h2 id="closing">Reflections</h2>
  <p>Back-office tools are used by merchants who need to get things done quickly every day. The point isn’t to fit in every feature, but to help them understand “where things stand right now” at a glance. The biggest challenge here was turning a complex set of calculation rules into an interface merchants can read: <span class="hl">the logic stays in the system; merchants only need to see the result.</span></p>
  <p>Looking back, there’s one thing I’d do differently: we didn’t plan event tracking for launch. Usage data for switching between the new week and month calendar views only started three months after launch, so today I can’t use numbers to show how well this redesign worked. On future projects, I’ll <span class="hl">decide at kickoff which data will show whether the design is working</span>.</p>
